ery  beginning the founding board members  wanted to make sure that this would be  open source scientific computing it  would not just represent the Python  tools but it would be open to open to  all tools as of today we have 18 and I  think we're you're missing one logo  waiting for one more of fiscal  sponsorship agreement to be signed 18  projects that we now physically sponsor  and our fiscal sponsorship besides just  being a legal entity we handle all the  financial admin that these projects need  and you would think that might not be a  lot but let me tell you it it is a lot  of work our staff is very very busy  trying to keep up with the the need that  this creates handling accounts invoicing  making sure anytime there's a hackathon  anytime projects are getting together  making sure all that's coordinated  trying to work on sustainability for the  projects making sure it research has  kept reproducible there's a lot of work  that goes into this and I I'm just  thrilled that we have so many wonderful  projects under our umbrella at this time  so at the same time our fiscal  sponsorship program was being worked on  there was also the issue of not only  supporting the projects but supporting  the community so there was a little  organization our conference that was  formed called PI data first of all I'm  Travis elefante Peter Wang and Julie  steel organized a workshop for 40 people  that took place at in Mountain View at  Google and they got to go together  talked about the Python scientific stack  and what the needs were at that time  continuum because at the same time  Travis and Peter were had founded  continuum analytics decided that they  would continue this on the larger scale  and had event at our first event in New  York late 2012 and I was because  continuum had donated office space for  none Focus which at that time was just  myself I helped out with that event and  during the event seeing the community  that it brought together it was decided  that 